Z||000000||Slammed Fiero||12-17-2000||12:49 AM||||I was reading on <A HREF="http://www.fierolt1.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fierolt1.com/</A>  that you have to move the entire engine assembly over 2" because of the distributor on the LT1  is this true on all year conversions?? This guy was using an archie kit as I plan to do , but his car is a Formula. I think the Lt1 is a great motor , i was going to go with an L98 , which my dad suggested , he has an 87 Vette , But I like the LT1 better because of the injection design and more power possibilities. and already higher Hp over the L98. <P>Thanks.<P>------------------<BR>Jonathan M McCreery<BR>86 GT<BR>17" Tr motorsports Typhoons Ground Control coil overs , Big bars front and rear , Mustang scoop , accel coil , no cat , 4spd , short shift , poly dog bone and bushings momo pedals and shift knob , round headlight conversion , Geni stainless steel oval exhaust tips. Pioneer 6000 CD player , Sony Mobile Amp. Currently doing big brake conversion...||||reg||1
Z||000001||Got2manyfieros||12-17-2000||01:22 AM||||In 1988, the shock towers were moved a couple of inches closer together to better accomodate the new suspention, this may be why the guy with the formula had to move everything over...||||reg||1
Z||000002||Archie||12-17-2000||03:35 AM||||The LT1 is a neat engine to have in a Fiero but you do have to move it over to the left in the car 2". A "normal" SBC engine (like that L98) Will fit into the engine compartment without moving it over BUT the LT1 & LT4 engines have a longer "Assembled length" than the "normal" engines. (The blocks the same length, but after you put on all the other parts & pulleys the LT1/4 is longer)<P>Because of this length you have to move it's initial location.<P>My advice, unless you absolutely, positively HAVE to have an LT1, use that L98.<P>Archie<p>[This message has been edited by Archie (edited 12-17-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||Slammed Fiero||12-17-2000||02:04 PM||||thanks!. It has been between an Lt1 , L98 and zz4..this narrows the decision..<BR><P>------------------<BR>Jonathan M McCreery<BR>86 GT<BR>17" Tr motorsports Typhoons Ground Control coil overs , Big bars front and rear , Mustang scoop , accel coil , no cat , 4spd , short shift , poly dog bone and bushings momo pedals and shift knob , round headlight conversion , Geni stainless steel oval exhaust tips. Pioneer 6000 CD player , Sony Mobile Amp. Currently doing big brake conversion...||||reg||1
Z||000004||fierov8||12-18-2000||02:06 PM||||An option if you want to go the L98 route:<P>Use the L98, ditch the TPI (runs outta breath in the upper RPM ranges) and go with the TPIS MiniRam and ZZ9 cam.  With head work (bigger valves, 5-angle, port work), you should be in the 375HP/375TQ neighborhood.  At least that's what the folks at TPIS are claiming.......<P>I'm doing the same, with help and advice from TPIS -- will let you know how it all goes together real soon -- engine is just about done and autox season starts in just a few months!  A trip to the chassis dyno will also be in order.  Just to keep the folks at TPIS honest........<P>Seems to be a proven combo - seen it spoken of highly on the Fbody and Ybody forums and lists.<P>-jeff d<BR>fort worth, TX||||reg||1
